  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 620 AM EDT Sat Aug 1 2026\n\nThe occluding surface low responsible for our active weather this \nweekend is centered over southeastern IA/NW IL as of this writing, \nwith a broad shield of rain showers and isolated storms extending \nfrom up near Milwaukee southeastward into Indy and KY/TN. A 500mb \ntrough encompasses the Great Lakes region, with the closed \nlow/associated speed max near the four corners. Ongoing steady rain \nshowers or drizzle extends from from Michigan City, IN into Marion, \nIN and is lifting northeast with time. Beyond that, light east-\nnortheast flow persists across the forecast area, with some light \nshower activity in Lower MI and near Lima, OH. \n\nAs the trough aloft/closed low digs southward into southern \nIN/KY through Sunday morning, the surface low drifts eastward, \ncentering around IND by 2 am EDT tonight (models still don't \nquite have the exact location pinned down). Shower activity will\nbecome more widespread through the afternoon into tonight as \nthe better upper level forcing arrives and several waves of \nprecip rotating around the surface low to our S-SE move through.\nMuch of the guidance has a persistent/recurrent rainfall along \nand south of US 30 today into tonight as the low pivots, and \nthen by Sunday a robust deformation zone extending from the low \nnear Indy (depends on the model) up into southeast Lower MI. The\nNAM really latches onto this, and it appears to be the furthest\nnorth of the solutions (centers low from Indy to near \nLogansport, IN). This keeps the axis from Logansport into \nHillsdale through much of Sunday. There is still lower \nconfidence as to whether this feature develops as robustly as \nindicated, and if it sinks southeastward with time (thus moving \nthe precip axis and distributing the rain) or stays in generally\nthe same location through Sun. If it stays stationary like the \nNAM depicts, some areas that see repeated thunderstorms or heavy\nrain could end up with 4 inches of rain or more. After a review\nof the latest guidance with the neighboring offices, decided \nnot to issue a flood watch given there are still questions as to\nwhere the band/earlier showers and storms set up and if they \nmove or stay relatively stationary into Sunday. \n\nThere is a marginal risk for isolated strong to severe storms this \nafternoon and evening in Indiana and Ohio, generally south of US 30. \nThe primary threat with any stronger storms would be damaging wind \ngusts to 60 mph. Models suggest around 500-1000 J/kg of surface CAPE \nthis afternoon, with 0-6 km bulk shear around 25-40 knots \nalong/south of US 30, which is plenty to work with in terms of \nstronger storm potential.\n\nOtherwise, low chance pops (20-40 percent) through the work week, \nwith highs in the 70s, low-mid 80s. \n\n\nHigh swim risk and beach hazards statement for La Porte County,\nIN and Southern Berrien County, MI from late this afternoon into\nMonday morning. Winds strengthen out of the northeast at 15 to\n25 knots and become more northerly through Sunday night. Waves\nof 1-2 feet today will build to around 3-5 feet by late this\nafternoon and evening (highest near New Buffalo, MI into \nMichigan City, IN). On Sunday, waves will build to 4 to 7 feet\nby the late afternoon. \n\nNorthern Berrien County, MI (including Silver Beach) will see\nlower wave heights today, generally 1 to 2 feet building to\naround 3 feet by late this afternoon/evening. Waves linger in\nthe 2-3 ft range until late Sunday afternoon, where they build\nto 3 to 5 feet. Have a moderate swim risk for today (into early\nSunday), and then a high swim risk for Sunday afternoon into\nMonday morning. The Beach Hazards Statement for this county is\nin effect starting late Sunday afternoon. \n\nExpect a strong southward flowing longshore current to develop\nwith the north-northeast flow later today into tonight and\npersist through Sunday, in addition to strong structural \ncurrents (particularly on the north sides of piers and \nbreakwalls). Rip currents are expected, especially where wave\nheights are greatest and flow is more northerly. If visiting the\nbeaches this weekend, monitor the beach forecast.",
